	abstract = {Chapter 3 compared two modes of contract enforcement:  self-governance, based on community information networks and ostracization or other forms of sanctions; and  formal state governance, based on a framework of laws and of auditing and monitoring mechanisms.  The first was limited by the matching and communication technologies; in large worlds it may fail altogether, or fail to realize the full economic benefits of comparative advantage. The second depended on the community’s ability to solve the collective-action problems of investing in the infrastructure of laws and monitoring, and to overcome the power of vested interests with sunk capital in the status},
