	isbn = {978-0-691-13034-7},
	url = {https://www.jstor.org/stable/j.ctt7t9hw.6},
	abstract = {The purpose of institutions and mechanisms of economic governance is to induce individuals to take cooperative or honest actions that achieve and sustain mutually beneficial outcomes in their economic interactions, countering the temptation of each individual to take opportunistic or cheating actions that promote his interest at the expense of the aggregate good. Similar issues arise in other fields, most prominently in evolutionary biology. Dugatkin (1999, pp. 17–27) gives a fourfold classification of the approaches to cooperation: (1) kin and family selection; (2) direct reciprocity; (3) selfish teamwork; and (4) group altruism.  The first of these is inherently biological.},
