	abstract = {This chapter examines the ways in which the Londoners attempted to minimise the ‘transaction costs’ of doing business in the city and to attract merchants and traders from abroad and within England. This involved maintaining the Thames waterway and road access, providing civic quays and markets, and ensuring a good food supply and hostelries. The city also developed efficient courts to deal with mercantile disputes and facilities for credit transfers and debt collection. In these ways the citizens created an attractive commercial environment which lengthened London's economic lead over provincial cities.},
